# ─── ONE HOME FOR THE SITE (must be first) ──────────────────
# The Netlify subdomain serves a full, indexable copy of the site. Without
# this, Google can index it as a rival website and links to it leak away.
https://lighthearted-sunburst-4f0ba2.netlify.app/*  https://foreverprint.com/:splat  301!

# Netlify URL rewriting (Phase 5 — landing pages + configurator)
#
# Format: /<from>  /<to>  <status>
#   Status 200 = rewrite (URL stays clean in browser)
#   Status 301 = permanent redirect (browser navigates to new URL)
#
# IMPORTANT: Netlify only applies these rules when no real file exists at the
# requested path. So /header.html, /footer.html etc. are still served from disk.
# Rules are processed top-to-bottom; the first match wins.

# ─── ORDER PATHS (must come BEFORE the slug catch-all) ──────
# /<slug>/order → configurator. The :slug placeholder captures the slug part
# and passes it to upload-and-print.html as a query param.
# E.g. /wedding-invitations/order → /upload-and-print.html?product=wedding-invitations

/:slug/order  /upload-and-print.html?product=:slug  200

# ─── KNOWN SITE PAGES (give them clean URLs) ────────────────
# Each line: clean URL → actual file. These come before the product catch-all.

# Only /admin needs a rule. Netlify serves a clean url from the matching .html
# file on its own — proven by /privacy, /terms, /returns, /delivery,
# /help-support and /guides, which have never had a rule and answer 200.
#
# The others used to be listed here, and that was the problem: it made each
# .html file the TARGET of a rewrite, so it could not be redirected away
# without the rewrite landing on a redirect. Removing them lets the .html form
# 301 to the clean one like every other page, which matters because Google may
# already have indexed the .html addresses.
/admin          /admin.html            200

# ─── ONE ADDRESS PER PAGE ───────────────────────────────────
# Every page answered on two urls — /privacy and /privacy.html both returned
# 200 with nothing choosing between them. Checked live on 6 October 2026: all
# 14 pairs tested returned 200 and none redirected. The canonical tag was the
# only thing telling Google which to index, and on five pages the site linked
# to the address the canonical disowned — 592 internal links pointing at urls
# we had asked Google to ignore.
#
# The site now uses clean urls everywhere: links, canonicals and the sitemap.
# These send the .html form to the clean one so there is a single address.
#
# The "!" matters. Netlify applies a rule only when no real file sits at the
# path, and privacy.html IS a real file, so without the force flag the rule
# would be ignored and the duplicate would stay.
#
# NOT listed here: contact, design-studio, how-it-works, products, track-order
# and upload-and-print. Something rewrites TO each of those .html files — most
# importantly /:slug/order, which every product's order page goes through. A
# forced redirect on a rewrite target risks sending that rewrite straight back
# out again, and breaking ordering is not worth tidying a url. Their links,
# canonicals and sitemap entries are already clean, which is what Google acts
# on; the redirect can follow once the behaviour is confirmed on the live site.
